grain grown in temperate climates globally. One of the first cultivated grains, it was domesticated in the Fertile Crescent around 9000 BC, giving it nonshattering spikelets and making it much easier to harvest. Its use then spread throughout Eurasia by 2000 BC. Barley prefers relatively low temperatures and well-drained soil to grow. It is relatively tolerant of drought and soil salinity, but is less winter-hardy than wheat or rye. Wild barley may refer to H. vulgare ssp. spontaneum, but may also refer to other species in the genus Hordeum."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-glutinous-barley"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glutinous_barley"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["South Korean cuisine","cuisine/south-korean-cuisine",[["Chalbori-ppang","dish/chalbori-ppang"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Red bean paste","ingredient/red-bean-paste"]]}]},"dish/grands-peres":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/grands-peres","n":"Grands-pères","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Cuisine of Quebec","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Grand-pères, grand-pères au sirop d'érable or grand-pères dans le sirop d'érable is a traditional dumpling based dessert in Québécois and Acadian cuisine. The term pépère is also used to describe this dish in some regions of Quebec like Beauce. This dumpling is commonly served during \"le temps des sucres\" in sugar shacks."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-grands-peres"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grands-p%C3%A8res"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Cuisine of Quebec","cuisine/cuisine-of-quebec"]]}]},"dish/harvest-moon-cafe":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/harvest-moon-cafe","n":"Harvest Moon Cafe","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from New American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Harvest Moon Cafe was a New American re